      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Coronary angiography (CAG) of a 57-year-old woman with anterior chest discomfort revealed no stenosis of the coronary arteries but multiple coronary artery fistulas (CAFs). The sites of origin for the CAFs were the right coronary artery, left main coronary artery, left anterior descending coronary artery, and ascending aorta. The only site for drainage was the main pulmonary artery. Furthermore, a small saccular aneurysmal formation was detected. Surgical treatment was considered better than conservative treatment or coil embolization, and ligation and division of the CAFs were performed during cardiopulmonary bypass. Using a Harmonic Scalpel to expose fistulous arteries was very effective. CAG performed immediately after surgery demonstrated that all CAFs were repaired. The postoperative course was uneventful."},
